You are {{AGENT}} doing a weekly hygiene pass. This stays local — do not message {{HUMAN}} from this job.

MEMORY CHECK: Before adding compact memory, run `{{MEMORY_CMD}} archive --apply`. This checks pressure, archives complete older entries at 80%, and preserves originals. If it reports no room can be freed, consolidate the oversized entry; never discard its history. Retrieve archived detail with `{{RECALL_CMD}}`.

1. Get the local date with `{{LIFE_CMD}} dates`.

2. CONTINUITY LOG ROTATION. Append-style logs grow without bound. Run:
   `{{ROTATE_CMD}} {{AUTONOMY_PATH}} --keep-days 7`
   That prints what would move without changing anything. If the report looks right, re-run with `--apply`.
   The tool moves older entries into `continuity/archive/<name>-YYYY-MM.md` and never deletes anything; it aborts and leaves the live file untouched if the entry count does not balance. Report sizes before and after.

3. JOURNAL ROTATION. The Lifelog is a lifetime record and nothing in it is ever deleted, but it
   should not grow into one enormous file either. Run:
   `{{ROTATE_CMD}} {{LIFELOG_PATH}} --keep-days 180`
   Same tool, same guarantees, much longer window: roughly the last six months stay in the live file
   where reflections can read them, and each older month moves to `{{SOUL_DIR}}/archive/Lifelog-YYYY-MM.md`.
   Dry run first, then `--apply` if the counts balance. If it reports entries it could not date, do not
   apply — say so instead: that means a daily entry was written without an ISO date in its heading, and
   the heading format needs fixing before rotation is safe.

4. WORKING FILES. Two directories hold disposable files and nothing else: the prose the model stages for
   `--file` flags, and Hermes's capture of each job's output. Run:
   `{{PRUNE_CMD}}`
   That reports what is older than seven days without removing anything. If the report looks right, re-run
   with `--apply`. These are the only two places anything is ever removed from; ledgers, logs, journals and
   images are archived instead, never pruned. Report the space freed.

5. CONTEXT BUDGET. Run `{{SELF_CMD}} soul --show`. If your headroom is negative or close to zero, say so plainly in your output — that is the signal to consolidate your SOUL block at the next weekly reflection, not to delete anything now.

6. OPEN LOOPS. Run `{{LOOPS_CMD}} list`. Close anything that is actually finished with
   `{{LOOPS_CMD}} close --id <id>`, and correct a `gentle_use` line that is no longer right by adding an
   update entry through `{{LOOPS_CMD}} add --file`. A stale open loop is worse than an absent one.
   Do not edit {{SOUL_DIR}}/ActiveContext.md: it is assembled from the state and this ledger by
   `{{ACTIVE_CMD}}`, and anything written there by hand is overwritten.

7. Verify the continuity hook still answers:
   `{{HOOK_TERMINAL_CMD}}` with `{}` supplied on standard input (or run `tamanitomo doctor`)
   It must print one JSON object. If it does not, report that as a blocker — the agent is running without continuity until it is fixed.

Do not change infrastructure, services, providers or packages from this job. Keep the output local and compact.
